Q2. Why pointers are not used in Java ?
memory safety - java manages memory automatically using garbage collection, avoiding issues like dangling pointer and memory leaks.
platform Independence - java uses references instead of pointers to ensure code runs consistently across different hardware architectures.
simplicity - java's design hides complexity  of direct memory management, making it easier to write and maintain the code.
security - By avoiding pointers java prevents unauthorized access to memory locations enhancing the security.

Q. What is 'JIT' compiler in java ?
JDK compilers java source code to byte code then byte code is converted to native machine  code which is understood by system.
JRE converts byte code to machine code for this conversion interpreter and JIT compiler are required.

Interpreter
interpreter interpret the code line by line and converts it into native machine code.
performance issue with interpreter
- Executing the same byte code repeatedly each time the program runs
- interpreter perform redundant work in interpreting the same instructions over and over
- interpreter typically lack the ability to store the result of previous execution.

JIT compiler
JIT compiler is a command of java runtime environment that improves the performance  of java application at runtime
the JIT compiler translates frequently executed or "hot" part of the byte code into native machine code at runtime.
after converting segment of bytecode to native machine code JIT compiler has the capability to store the resulting native code in cache.
if program encounters the same segment during it's execution it can reuse the cached native code.

JIT compiler is part of JVM that converts frequently used bytecode into fast native machine code while programming is running.
JVM start by interpreting the code line by line
if it finds some part of running many times (hotspot), it uses JIT to compile them into faster machine code.
next time the code runs it uses the faster version.

Q4. What is Write Once Run Anywhere concept in Java ?
java compiler converts source code to byte code, byte code  comprises instructions for the java virtual machine and is not bound to any specific hardware or OS.
the compiler byte code is executable on any device  with compatible JVM .

Q5. What is 'marker interface' in java ?
A marker interface is a interface that does  not declare any method.
basically empty interface, it is also known as tag interface
ex: Serializable, cloneable, Remote interface
a. Serializable
JDK uses this interface in such a way that, NotSerializableException is thrown if item to be serialized does not implement serializable interface.

b. Cloneable
JDK uses this interface in such a way that, CloneNotSupportedException is thrown if item to be cloned does not implement cloneable interface.

Uses of marker interface
marker interface indicates some special characteristics or features associated with the implementing class.
we deign custom marker interface in java for specific application requirement.

Q6. How to make class immutable in Java ?
immutable class in java are those classes who's instances cannot be modified
immutable classes in java are String and all wrapper classes.

To make class immutable
. Make the class final
. mark all the fields private and final
. remove setter methods
. make constructor initialize all fields
. avoid returning mutable objects


